# Fixtures tools/verify-expected.sh does NOT check against node.
#
# The gate exists because "node is the oracle" was an honor-system rule. Every
# exemption is a hole in it, so each one is argued here rather than buried in a
# comment at the top of a fixture where nobody reads it. Format:
#
#   <path>    <reason>
#
# Two kinds live here, and they are not the same:
#   NOT-RUNNABLE — node cannot execute the fixture at all, or its output is not
#                  a function of the program (paths, pids, benchmarks).
#   DIVERGENCE   — node runs it fine and milojs disagrees. The .expected encodes
#                  the milojs answer on purpose. These are open bugs with the
#                  fixture passing; do not add one without a backlog entry.

# --- NOT-RUNNABLE ---

tests/binaryLength.js    calls __byteLength, a milojs engine intrinsic with no node equivalent
tests/membench.js        peak-RSS benchmark; the headline number is /usr/bin/time -l, not stdout

# --- DIVERGENCE (open bugs) ---

tests/promises.js        await of an ALREADY-SETTLED promise resumes inline instead of after a
#                        microtask tick, so chain() completes before its .then() attaches and
#                        "then 42" prints first; node prints it seventh. Every other line and
#                        value matches node exactly.
tests/radixToString.js   Number.prototype.toString(radix) emits QuickJS/JSC shortest-round-trip
#                        digits, not node's: (1.3).toString(7) is 1.204620462046204621 here and
#                        1.2046204620462046205 under node. The .expected is a QuickJS capture,
#                        which is the one place in this suite where node is deliberately not the
#                        oracle.
tests/errorInspect.js    console.log(new Error("boom")) prints "Error: boom"; node prints the
#                        message followed by the full stack, because milojs Errors carry no
#                        stack property yet.
